diff --git a/.agents/skills/stuck-crewmate-recovery/SKILL.md b/.agents/skills/stuck-crewmate-recovery/SKILL.md
index b366ab8..2edc5ee 100644
--- a/.agents/skills/stuck-crewmate-recovery/SKILL.md
+++ b/.agents/skills/stuck-crewmate-recovery/SKILL.md
@@ -78,6 +78,9 @@ Only positive socket refusal or absence is a daemon-down finding; escalate that
 
 Escalate in order:
 
+0. If `state/<id>.llmg.json` exists (written by the llm-graphs omp plugin), read it before the pane: it names the active step, done/blocked/total counts, the last tool and when it ran, queued asks, inbox messages handled, and the paths of the worker's `plan.md` and report.
+   A recent `last_tool_at` with an active step means the worker is busy, not wedged; a blocked step with a reason is the blocker to act on.
+   Read `plan.md` when you need the evidence behind a step. Fall back to the pane when the file is absent or older than the last status line.
 1. Peek the pane, and check the task's steering inbox (`state/<id>.inbox/`) for unhandled `*.msg` records - a stale wake naming an unread firstmate instruction means the worker never acknowledged a durable steer, and the record itself shows exactly what was intended.
    A worker whose current state is a declared `paused:` wait has only said what it is waiting for, so verify the condition it names - its own background job's terminal record or process, for example - and when that condition has already cleared, steer the worker to read the result and resume instead of reporting the wait as healthy.
 2. If the crewmate is waiting on a question its brief already answers, follow the [T3 pending-request procedure](../../../docs/t3code-backend.md#push-events-and-polling-fallback) for a T3 runtime request.
diff --git a/bin/fm-brief.sh b/bin/fm-brief.sh
index fab4f16..d2c89c0 100755
--- a/bin/fm-brief.sh
+++ b/bin/fm-brief.sh
@@ -476,6 +476,7 @@ Firstmate steers you through durable message files in $INBOX_DIR.
 When a terminal message says an instruction is waiting there - and at any natural checkpoint when you are unsure - list them with \`find $INBOX_DIR -maxdepth 1 -name '*.msg' 2>/dev/null | sort -V\`, read the complete contents of each record (including its final line even when it is not newline-terminated) and act on each message in numeric order, then acknowledge each handled message by moving it: \`mv $INBOX_DIR/NNN.msg $INBOX_DIR/handled/\`.
 Prefer the format-aware \`fm_task_inbox_body\` reader from \`bin/fm-task-inbox-lib.sh\` when it is available. If you read manually, a header line \`body-terminator=1\` means the writer added exactly one synthetic final newline to terminate the record file; that marked newline is not part of the logical message body, so remove only it and preserve any intentional trailing newlines. Legacy records without that marker may end without a newline; read them through the file end.
 The move IS the acknowledgement: without it firstmate rings again and eventually treats you as stuck. An empty or absent inbox needs no action.
+If a tool in your session has already delivered a Firstmate message to you and says it is acknowledged, it is already in \`handled/\`; do not look for it again. If a tool says Firstmate has already been told you are blocked, the \`blocked:\` line is written; do not append a second one.
 EOF
 if [ -e "$CONFIG/wait-no-turns" ]; then
   INBOX_SECTION+="Do not poll or list the inbox while waiting; a waiting instruction rings."$'\n'
